NAME
       tui - full-screen terminal helper

SYNOPSIS
       #include <tui.h>

       void tui_begin(void);
       void tui_end(void);
       void tui_size(int *rows, int *cols);
       void tui_clear(void);
       void tui_move(int row, int col);
       void tui_hide_cursor(void);
       void tui_show_cursor(void);
       void tui_puts(const char *s);
       void tui_home(void);
       int  tui_read_key(void);

DESCRIPTION
       tui_begin puts the terminal in raw mode, switches to the
       alternate screen and hides the cursor; tui_end undoes all three,
       which restores the shell and its scrollback exactly as it was.

       tui_size asks the terminal for its size, which the console
       recomputes when the font changes.

       tui_read_key decodes escape sequences and returns TK_UP,
       TK_DOWN, TK_LEFT, TK_RIGHT, TK_HOME, TK_END, TK_PGUP, TK_PGDN,
       TK_DEL and TK_RESIZE for the special keys. It also normalises
       what the keyboard actually sends: Enter arrives as a newline
       rather than a carriage return, and Backspace as a backspace
       rather than delete, so compare against TK_ENTER and TK_BACKSP.

       All output goes through stdio, so mixing tui calls with printf
       keeps its order.

       A read interrupted by a signal is reported as TK_RESIZE rather
       than as end of input, so a program that redraws on TK_RESIZE
       also repaints when the console tells it the geometry changed or
       when it gets the screen back after a console switch.

NOTES
       tui_read_key blocks. To animate while waiting, wait with poll(2)
       on descriptor 0 for the frame time and call tui_read_key only
       once poll reports input.

SEE ALSO
       curses(3), termios(3), poll(2), cfm(1), neo(1)
